# Gantry — The assets you produce

Gantry ships with no pre-made models or sound. The machine this project is
built on carries asset tools on its `PATH`, and the build produces every model
and sound the game uses with them, commits the produced files, and wires them
in. This file is the contract: what to produce, which tool produces it, and
how the game consumes it.

Production is a one-time step. The tools are development tools of this
machine; the committed files are the assets, and the build bundles them, so
`npm ci` and `npm run build` invoke no tool and the built site fetches nothing
from outside its own `dist/`.

Commit the produced files under `assets/`, at these paths: each model as
`assets/models/<model>.glb`, under the model name the table below gives it; each
cue's sound as `assets/audio/<cue>.wav`, under the cue name `specs/ui.md` gives
it; and the music bed as `assets/audio/music.wav` with `assets/audio/music.mid`
beside it. The name a file carries is what says which subject or which cue it
is.

## The tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `voxel` | a voxel model, meshed to a `.glb` | the crane's parts and the loads |
| `sfx-synth` | a procedural sound → `.wav` | the cues |
| `sfx-sample` | a sampled sound over the sample pack → `.wav` | the cues |
| `music` | sequenced music over the instrument bank → `.wav` + `.mid` | the music bed |

Each is a command-line tool, and `<tool> --help` states its operations
(`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags). In outline, each
records the operations you run and then renders the finished file: `voxel`
sculpts an opaque-color voxel volume and its `render` meshes it to the `.glb`
the game loads, drawing a preview PNG beside it to check your work; the audio
tools record voices, layers, or notes and render a PCM `.wav`. `sfx-sample`
draws on the `combat-core` sample pack and `music` on the `gm-lite` instrument
bank, both present in the container and browsed with `list-samples` and
`list-instruments`; `music` emits a portable `.mid` beside the `.wav` you play.

## The models

Sculpt each model with `voxel` at `VOXELS_PER_UNIT` (`8`): eight voxels to one
world unit, so the game draws every model at a scale of `1 / VOXELS_PER_UNIT`.
Produce these, each committed as `assets/models/<model>.glb` under the name in
the first column:

| Model | Stands for | Sized about |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ring | the slew ring: a squat bearing drum between its flanges | `2.5 x 2 x 2.5` units |
| trolley | the carriage on the rail | `1.5 x 1 x 1.5` units |
| hook | the hook block at the cable's end | `0.6 x 1 x 0.6` units |
| counterweight | a dense ballast block | `1.5 x 1.5 x 1.5` units |
| mount | an anchor's ground fixture | `1.5 x 0.75 x 1.5` units |
| crate | the `crate` load class | its class box, `2 x 2 x 2` units |
| container | the `container` load class | its class box, `4 x 2 x 2` units |
| drum | the `drum` load class | its class box, `2 x 3 x 2` units |

The three load models fill their class boxes: a load's collisions and
placement use the class dimensions from `specs/world.md` whatever is drawn, so
a model well short of its box would read as missing what it visibly overlaps.
The part models are sized to sit believably on the structure; the figures
above are the intent, not a tolerance. Art direction is yours: one coherent
machine across the parts, loads that read at a glance as a crate, a container,
and a drum, and forms that read from every camera angle, since the player
orbits freely.

The game draws each model wherever its subject is: the ring centered on the
slew axis between its flanges and turning with the arm, the trolley on the
track at the trolley position, the hook at the bob turned to the grip's yaw,
a counterweight at each carrying node, a mount at each anchor, and each load
at its pose, waiting, hanging, or placed.

## Consuming a model

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
`@clockwyrks/voxel-runtime` is already a dependency of the project; import
it like any other. Decode each committed `.glb` with its `parseGlb`, which
returns the mesh as plain typed arrays with per-vertex colors, and render it
through the `three` binding, `@clockwyrks/voxel-runtime/three`, whose
`buildPartGeometry` wraps the mesh into a `BufferGeometry` for the scene. The
package's own types are the authoritative API. `three` is the project's, so
the runtime shares the build's single instance.

Reference every asset page-relative, never by a root-absolute URL: the built
site is served from a sub-path as well as from a root, so import each `.glb`
and `.wav` through the bundler (or resolve it with `new URL(...,
import.meta.url)`) and use the URL that comes back. The project's Vite config
already emits relative URLs.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
The {{engine.name}} engine loads the models and decodes them. Its asset loader
resolves every path under one root, `ASSET_ROOT` (`assets/`), relative to the
page the build is served from, and its model loader decodes glTF 2.0, which is
what `voxel` writes, so a committed `.glb` comes back as the node tree, meshes,
and per-vertex colors the file carries. Load each model through that loader
rather than fetching a URL of your own, writing the path relative to that root,
and place a copy of what comes back in the engine's scene for each subject it
draws. No part of the build decodes glTF itself, and nothing else fetches an
asset.

A path the loader resolves reaches the file at whatever base path the site is
served from, but only where the built site carries that file under the root:
the supplied toolchain does not copy `assets/` into `dist/`, so the built
output carrying every produced file under that root is the build's to arrange.
The engine's own documentation is the authoritative API.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine loads the models and decodes them. Its asset loader
resolves every path under one root, `ASSET_ROOT` (`assets/`), relative to the
page the build is served from, and its model loader decodes glTF 2.0, which is
what `voxel` writes, so a committed `.glb` comes back as the node tree, meshes,
and per-vertex colors the file carries. Load each model through that loader
rather than fetching a URL of your own, writing the path relative to that root,
and draw it with a model component, which clones the loaded model onto the
actor it belongs to, so one load backs every copy of that part on screen. No
part of the build decodes glTF itself, and nothing else fetches an asset.

A path the loader resolves reaches the file at whatever base path the site is
served from, but only where the built site carries that file under the root:
the supplied toolchain does not copy `assets/` into `dist/`, so the built
output carrying every produced file under that root is the build's to arrange.
The engine's own documentation is the authoritative API.
{{/if}}

## The sound

Produce one `.wav` per cue in `specs/ui.md`, committed as
`assets/audio/<cue>.wav`, with `sfx-synth` and `sfx-sample` as suits each:

| Cue | Tool | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `place` | `sfx-synth` | A part seating: a short, dry clack. |
| `delete` | `sfx-synth` | A part coming away, lighter than `place`. |
| `run-start` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | The yard's signal that the tape is about to run. |
| `attach` | `sfx-sample` | The clunk of the hook taking a load. |
| `placed` | `sfx-sample` | A load's weight settling onto its pad. |
| `creak` | `sfx-sample` | Steel complaining under load. |
| `break` | `sfx-sample` | A member letting go: a hard snap. |
| `collapse` | `sfx-sample` | The whole crane coming down, with real weight. |
| `complete` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | The site cleared. |
| `fail` | `sfx-synth` | The run ended badly. |
| `motor` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | The drive turning, authored to loop cleanly. |

Produce the music bed with `music`: a steady, unhurried machine-yard piece
under the title and select screens, committed as `assets/audio/music.wav` with
the `.mid` it emits beside it as `assets/audio/music.mid`.

Sound reads the yard's character, so hold it to the same bar as the models:
cues that are felt rather than noticed, a `collapse` with real weight, and a
loop that does not fatigue at four-times watch speed.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Load each `.wav` the same page-relative way as every other produced file,
decode it with the Web Audio API, and play it on the event `specs/ui.md`
names, `motor` as the one loop. Sound does not start before the player's first
interaction with the page, and `mute` silences the whole bus.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
Bind each `.wav` to its cue name through the engine's audio, which loads and
decodes the file through the same asset loader, and play the cue by name on
the event `specs/ui.md` names, `motor` as the one loop the engine starts and
stops. The engine owns the first-interaction unlock and the mute bit, which
the state mirrors (`specs/state.md`).
{{else}}
Bind each `.wav` to its cue name through the engine's audio, which loads and
decodes the file through the same asset loader, and play the cue by name from
the world on the event `specs/ui.md` names, `motor` as the one loop the world
starts and stops. The engine owns the first-interaction unlock and the mute
bit, which the state mirrors (`specs/state.md`).
{{/if}}

## What is drawn in code

Everything else on screen is drawn in code, with no produced file behind it:
the yard's ground, sky, and light; the lattice and envelope aids; the members,
each as real drawn geometry telling strut, cable, and rail apart, colored by
utilization during a run; the hoist cable; the pads and their yaw marks; every
readout, menu, and screen; and the debug overlay. The produced models sit in
that scene; the scene itself is the build's.
